-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/85887346-8e3d-4ff7-85cc-6b141409796d
-- title:
--   A wanted-item execution combines the dense guard with terminal search
-- statement:
--   Let $w$ be a key-consistent wanted item for raw incidences $I$, and fix $K\le J$. Suppose memory stores exact source stages, all required catalogs, decoded output rows, occurrence and cover data, and residue buckets and values for physical arrays $L,R$. The retained counter stores $o$ and its threshold stores $\theta$; let $m$ be the actual root mass.
--
--   Assume the stated source and catalog bounds, separated counter/row/frontier and terminal layouts, query-scratch contracts, and terminal resource bounds. In particular $o+m+\theta+2$, all values and targets, and $N_LN_R$ fit words; $S\le W$, and the depth allowance is $K+2(512hJ)+|\mathcal F_K|+N_T+27$, where $N_T=\binom{2h(J-K)}{h(J-K)}$. Execution returns a Boolean with
--   $$r=1\iff\theta<o+m\ \lor\ \exists u\in\mathcal F_K,\ \operatorname{TerminalHit}(u),\qquad\nu(\mathrm{counter})=o+m.$$
--   It preserves memory below packed rows except the counter. Time is initialization plus $700$, with the search budget added only if the guard does not fire.
--
--   This is the complete guarded contract for one wanted item.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/LightReportingWanted.lean#L49-L209

